            '{"parser": "legacy-migration", "file_name": "סיכום למבחן אחזור מידע (1).pdf", "file_type": "pdf", "legacyData": {"legacy_id": "2d41e1fd-da2d-4073-8389-da32985fb508", "external_id": null, "collection": "collection", "type": "chunk-data", "tags": "", "user_id": "de0ff05457533c93fdf3e0d1cdd0f808", "create_date": "2025-08-26T11:29:00.308Z", "link_to_file": "https://jeenfilesdevweu.blob.core.windows.net/jeendocs/de0ff05457533c93fdf3e0d1cdd0f808/data/1756207659351-0c8cd1917d00e951630029e4978e9147.pdf", "excerptKeywords": "KEYWORDS: Jaccard, שכיחות, מושג, מטריצה, מסמך"}}'::jsonb,
            '2025-08-26T11:29:00.308000+00:00',
            $TRANS$We probably won't use the Jaccard metric because it's not sophisticated enough because it doesn't address the frequency of a concept in a document, the rarity of a concept in the document, and the length of the document. Frequency of the term in a document Let's look at the following matrix: 5 1 (2,( 6 1 (1,) = = DQueryJC DQueryJC The matrix contains the names of the documents and checks whether the words appear in those documents (1 exists, 0 does not exist). The check of whether a word exists or not is a binary vector that is as long as the number of concepts in the dictionary. We will expand the matrix to a count matrix (a matrix that measures the amount of word appearing in a document).$TRANS$
        );
