            'text'::chunks_content_type_enum,
            NULL,
            459,
            81,
            '{"parser": "legacy-migration", "file_name": "סיכום למבחן אחזור מידע (1).pdf", "file_type": "pdf", "legacyData": {"legacy_id": "7aff1250-17ce-4e07-89ac-4cce92ed10c4", "external_id": null, "collection": "collection", "type": "chunk-data", "tags": "", "user_id": "de0ff05457533c93fdf3e0d1cdd0f808", "create_date": "2025-08-26T11:29:00.270Z", "link_to_file": "https://jeenfilesdevweu.blob.core.windows.net/jeendocs/de0ff05457533c93fdf3e0d1cdd0f808/data/1756207659351-0c8cd1917d00e951630029e4978e9147.pdf", "excerptKeywords": "KEYWORDS: רווח, tokens, גרש, נורמליזציה, שפות שונות"}}'::jsonb,
            '2025-08-26T11:29:00.270000+00:00',
            $TRANS$The most trivial idea is to use space as a separator for tokens, or commas. Situations that require special attention: - apostrophe: aren't vs. Mr O'neill - Space: Usually a separation between words, but sometimes found in names such as city names. - Hyphens – Link words like are, as, and – different languages. In Chinese and Japanese, there are no spaces between the words. • Linguistic Modules – There is a process called normalization in which we want to bring our words to a basic and uniform form so that the words USA and U.S.A. converge into the same form. - Lowering hyphens, periods.$TRANS$
