            '{"parser": "legacy-migration", "file_name": "סיכום למבחן אחזור מידע (1).pdf", "file_type": "pdf", "legacyData": {"legacy_id": "9b29f35d-6120-47eb-9b6d-598cdc1265be", "external_id": null, "collection": "collection", "type": "chunk-data", "tags": "", "user_id": "de0ff05457533c93fdf3e0d1cdd0f808", "create_date": "2025-08-26T11:29:00.294Z", "link_to_file": "https://jeenfilesdevweu.blob.core.windows.net/jeendocs/de0ff05457533c93fdf3e0d1cdd0f808/data/1756207659351-0c8cd1917d00e951630029e4978e9147.pdf", "excerptKeywords": "KEYWORDS: שגיאה, תיקון, אפשרויות, אינדקס, זיכרון"}}'::jsonb,
            '2025-08-26T11:29:00.294000+00:00',
            $TRANS$So let's assume that the error was made only in the first word. There are 4 suggestions for repair. We will check their prevalence. We will repeat the same process only for the second word and then only for the third word. For example, Dan Good, let's say Dan has 3 options to fix and Good has 2 options to fix. If he made a mistake in both words, all the options should be checked, i.e. 2 * 3 = 6. But it can be assumed that he was wrong with only one of the words: if he was wrong only with Dan, there will be 2 possibilities. If he made a mistake only in good, there will be 3 possibilities. In other words, a total of 5 options. Building an Index What happens when there is no memory space for the list of assignments?$TRANS$
        );
