            '{"parser": "legacy-migration", "file_name": "סיכום למבחן אחזור מידע (1).pdf", "file_type": "pdf", "legacyData": {"legacy_id": "9e0b1c94-dfdd-4284-8dd0-b2dccc6be49c", "external_id": null, "collection": "collection", "type": "chunk-data", "tags": "", "user_id": "de0ff05457533c93fdf3e0d1cdd0f808", "create_date": "2025-08-26T11:29:00.367Z", "link_to_file": "https://jeenfilesdevweu.blob.core.windows.net/jeendocs/de0ff05457533c93fdf3e0d1cdd0f808/data/1756207659351-0c8cd1917d00e951630029e4978e9147.pdf", "excerptKeywords": "KEYWORDS: מנוע, מסמכים, תוצאות, חיפוש, מדד"}}'::jsonb,
            '2025-08-26T11:29:00.367000+00:00',
            $TRANS$We would like to argue that the engine that returned more documents has a better chance of getting a better score than the search engine that returned fewer documents. The more results we accumulate, the higher the index will be, so it makes sense that an engine that returns more documents will get a bigger result. Let's demonstrate: Let's take two search engines: Search Engine 1: NDCG(DCG/IDCG) 1 DCG Rel/Log Log Rel Doc no. 1 1.0 - - 1.0 1 1 1.8 0.8 1 0.8 2 1 2.11 0.31 1.58 0.5 3 1 2.16 0.005 2.0 0.1 4 Search Engine 2: NDCG(DCG/IDCG) DCG Rel/Log Log Rel Doc no.$TRANS$
